 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Delivery publishes committed revisions over sockets the board owns, and browsers reconnect and catch up from their last seen revision. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected as simplest stateless operation but slower (seconds of lag) and read-heavy, and because it pushes a polling loop into browsers; a hosted realtime service was rejected as the quickest route to low latency but carrying recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting instead accepts connection-capacity and reconnect work to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
